1
00:00:02,187 --> 00:00:04,429
Everybody's craving, maybe.

2
00:00:04,769 --> 00:00:13,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Like I said today, that craving... Understanding craving was seeing...

3
00:00:14,475 --> 00:00:16,317
[SPEAKER_01]: I was seeing it as very unattractive.

4
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:25,383
[SPEAKER_01]: I started off with a super reflection and came down to what is unattractive in all things, as craving in regard to

5
00:00:26,578 --> 00:00:29,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That is disgusting because of the things that it does.

6
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:31,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that it implies.

7
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that implies that you get caught up in.

8
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:33,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

9
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:35,581
[SPEAKER_01]: The self, the omniscience, everything.

10
00:00:35,681 --> 00:00:35,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

11
00:00:36,341 --> 00:00:38,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So cravings is unattractive.

12
00:00:40,763 --> 00:00:42,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is how it should be, really.

13
00:00:42,223 --> 00:00:47,565
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the things, no matter what approach you take, it will end up leading to the same conclusions.

14
00:00:49,606 --> 00:00:51,467
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like, oh, I don't have true cognizance.

15
00:00:51,547 --> 00:00:52,447
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just whatever.

16
00:00:52,467 --> 00:00:55,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people have become skilled in sense-based, skilled in this, skilled in that.

17
00:00:56,139 --> 00:00:58,441
[SPEAKER_00]: All for the purpose of just revealing the same things.

18
00:01:00,883 --> 00:01:07,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not going to develop any new insights.

19
00:01:08,408 --> 00:01:12,591
[SPEAKER_00]: You might develop new aspects of the same insights, depending on what approaches you take.

20
00:01:13,932 --> 00:01:21,158
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, you just to pursue whichever aspect you acquire, till the end, till the craving is gone, and you're another.

21
00:01:25,205 --> 00:01:26,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all delighting.

22
00:01:27,106 --> 00:01:35,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, it's kind of inseparable from what we spoke about the delight and the nature of craving.

23
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:40,817
[SPEAKER_00]: If you take delight for granted, you crave.

24
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:48,602
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't even see the delight as phenomenon, the reason, what is delight?

25
00:01:48,863 --> 00:01:53,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Delight, that joy that arises upon... that's all that it means craving is there.

26
00:01:57,133 --> 00:01:57,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So the...

27
00:01:58,153 --> 00:02:16,701
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the important thing is to not... well, not fall into a trap of thinking linearly, like, oh, first I felt, then I had the attitude of craving, then I'm delighting, then I must holding, then I get attached, then whatever it is.

28
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:16,941
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

29
00:02:17,841 --> 00:02:21,763
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all like within this... when this receives, it's all parthita upādāna.

30
00:02:22,685 --> 00:02:28,490
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might describe different aspects differently, but the point of it is they're simultaneously present.

31
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:31,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, presence of feeling means presence of craving, for a Bhutujana.

32
00:02:37,437 --> 00:02:48,186
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can think of it, like, even in terms of, again, you can think of it even in terms of, um, just, like, when we spoke just a couple of hours ago,

33
00:02:54,069 --> 00:03:04,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Keeping yourself bound to a domain of sensuality makes you liable to whatever arises in that domain.

34
00:03:05,377 --> 00:03:11,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, keeping yourself bound to the domain of feelings makes yourself liable to whatever feelings arise.

35
00:03:16,304 --> 00:03:21,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you don't necessarily go after your feelings, the fact that you feel

36
00:03:23,111 --> 00:03:24,472
[SPEAKER_00]: That feeling is appropriate.

37
00:03:24,532 --> 00:03:25,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is mine.

38
00:03:25,452 --> 00:03:26,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is me.

39
00:03:26,213 --> 00:03:27,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is I am.

40
00:03:28,774 --> 00:03:34,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you liable to the other possibilities of that feeling that you're appropriating now.

41
00:03:34,918 --> 00:03:37,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when that changes, you won't be able to escape it.

42
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:40,341
[SPEAKER_00]: When that feeling changes, you won't be able to escape that change.

43
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:41,882
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you'll be affected by it.

44
00:03:42,678 --> 00:03:47,319
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, if feeling changes for even more pleasure, you won't be able to remain indifferent.

45
00:03:47,359 --> 00:03:48,039
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be pulled.

46
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:49,640
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be hooked even further.

47
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:52,741
[SPEAKER_00]: If feeling changes to its pain, you'll be suffering.

48
00:03:52,761 --> 00:03:53,501
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be affected.

49
00:03:54,041 --> 00:03:59,503
[SPEAKER_00]: And the fact that you're affected in the first place, that's what all... that's already what suffering is.

50
00:03:59,623 --> 00:04:00,463
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what dukkha is.

51
00:04:01,583 --> 00:04:08,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the possibility... the fact that you won't be able to escape the possibility when it arises, that's dukkha.

52
00:04:09,485 --> 00:04:09,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

53
00:04:09,725 --> 00:04:11,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have no control over possibilities.

54
00:04:12,277 --> 00:04:13,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

55
00:04:14,258 --> 00:04:17,160
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense of control can only be exercised in regard to what's actual.

56
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:25,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can start, like, thinking about probabilities and coefficients and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, just to give you a sense of control.

57
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:33,189
[SPEAKER_01]: In reality, possibility always remains a possibility, which means it can always be otherwise, which means your control is useless there.

58
00:04:34,827 --> 00:04:39,910
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is, you're so used to control, that you always see possibilities as secondary, as optional.

59
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:44,513
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, fundamentally, structurally speaking, they come before the actual thing.

60
00:04:45,814 --> 00:04:57,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, possibilities, not being able to control them, yet depending upon them, that's what the nature of sankara is, exactly that very thing.

61
00:04:58,621 --> 00:05:01,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It determines everything, yet you have no say in it.

62
00:05:02,324 --> 00:05:03,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't determine it.

63
00:05:04,085 --> 00:05:04,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

64
00:05:04,944 --> 00:05:09,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, whatever you can determine, whatever you can determine, whatever you can intend, will be actual.

65
00:05:09,665 --> 00:05:11,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, there's always something possible in relation to that.

66
00:05:12,006 --> 00:05:16,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, you can never, like, uproot it through your intention.

67
00:05:16,508 --> 00:05:18,508
[SPEAKER_00]: You can never take it up and really own it.

68
00:05:20,409 --> 00:05:27,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the act of owning is already determined by the possibilities of ownership, which are not yours and cannot be owned.

69
00:05:31,732 --> 00:05:34,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding of feelings gets you out of the domain of

70
00:05:37,824 --> 00:05:46,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you still feel, but your view or your relationship is completely changed to it.

71
00:05:47,006 --> 00:05:50,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then you can't really say that you feel anymore.

72
00:05:50,207 --> 00:05:56,969
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just say feeling is there, but it's not... doesn't exist as my feeling, which is what existence is.

73
00:06:08,197 --> 00:06:09,478
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to imagine how it is.

74
00:06:09,758 --> 00:06:13,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be like in death, and then atom and separation.

75
00:06:14,521 --> 00:06:17,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think my self would be cut.

76
00:06:19,584 --> 00:06:21,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you feel it?

77
00:06:21,585 --> 00:06:22,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Self is cut.

78
00:06:22,405 --> 00:06:27,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when you look from the sense of... from the point of view of self, it feels like death.

79
00:06:28,589 --> 00:06:33,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't even comprehend how can something be without existing.

80
00:06:37,008 --> 00:06:39,089
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, as a practitioner you have to die.

81
00:06:39,429 --> 00:06:40,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Practitioner has to die.

82
00:06:52,935 --> 00:06:53,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And back on to the craving.

83
00:06:58,337 --> 00:06:59,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inseparable from the feeling.

84
00:06:59,958 --> 00:07:03,920
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just examine craving as if an inseparable.

85
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:14,255
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see the nature, you can distinguish, you can discern the nature, but it's simultaneously present with the feeling.

86
00:07:19,798 --> 00:07:33,525
[SPEAKER_00]: But in order to discern the nature, to start discerning the particular difference between different types of craving, and I don't mean psychologically examining them, I just mean in pleasure,

87
00:07:34,056 --> 00:07:37,699
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to recognize the nature of craving in regard to the pleasure.

88
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:50,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, like, theoretically start pondering on what craving is, but to see it really in regard to the presently arisen pleasure.

89
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:54,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And same with pain, and same with neutral feeling.

90
00:07:58,355 --> 00:08:02,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is, when there's a pleasant feeling, people would dismiss the entire thing.

91
00:08:03,486 --> 00:08:05,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Throw the baby out with the bathwater.

92
00:08:07,648 --> 00:08:09,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the degree of it is not a problem.

93
00:08:09,528 --> 00:08:10,489
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just pleasant.

94
00:08:10,589 --> 00:08:11,409
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just agreeable.

95
00:08:13,010 --> 00:08:14,791
[SPEAKER_00]: What else there is what you do.

96
00:08:15,291 --> 00:08:16,411
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you should stop doing.

97
00:08:20,233 --> 00:08:22,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through feeling, you can recognize the craving.

98
00:08:24,334 --> 00:08:25,455
[SPEAKER_00]: With feeling, craving.

99
00:08:30,297 --> 00:08:32,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why it's bhakti-bhakti, some part of description.

100
00:08:35,082 --> 00:08:38,963
[SPEAKER_00]: While feeling craving to be even more accurate.

101
00:08:40,684 --> 00:08:43,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, we say, when people start thinking, looking left and right...

102
00:08:43,504 --> 00:08:46,865
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see yourself forgetting, feeling, collision, right?

103
00:08:47,946 --> 00:08:48,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

104
00:08:48,266 --> 00:08:50,426
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to, kind of, reverse-engineer.

105
00:08:52,047 --> 00:08:54,727
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to recognize that you are... OK, I'm a kutujana.

106
00:08:55,648 --> 00:08:58,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, when I feel like, great, you don't see any of it.

107
00:09:00,169 --> 00:09:02,049
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the first thought.

108
00:09:02,089 --> 00:09:03,910
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the starting point.

109
00:09:05,555 --> 00:09:07,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I feel, I crave.

110
00:09:09,718 --> 00:09:14,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, now the first reaction might be, oh, I must not feel.

111
00:09:15,422 --> 00:09:16,442
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the problem.

112
00:09:17,143 --> 00:09:18,284
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you crave.

113
00:09:20,665 --> 00:09:22,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So how can you feel without craving?

114
00:09:26,509 --> 00:09:28,170
[SPEAKER_00]: If you find that out, you're free from craving.

115
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:29,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving becomes redundant.

116
00:09:29,471 --> 00:09:30,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can feel,

117
00:09:31,327 --> 00:09:36,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling arises irregardless of craving.

118
00:09:41,029 --> 00:09:43,750
[SPEAKER_00]: By not knowing that craving is redundant, you crave.

119
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:48,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but it's a pointless thing.

120
00:09:49,051 --> 00:09:51,731
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a pointless endeavour.

121
00:09:51,951 --> 00:09:53,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Unnecessary.

122
00:10:08,325 --> 00:10:09,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, it's fair, also.

123
00:10:10,325 --> 00:10:16,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, it's just because, yeah, it... there is a thing is indifferent to the favour.

124
00:10:20,388 --> 00:10:21,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the thing arises and ceases.

125
00:10:22,609 --> 00:10:24,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you crave or not, makes no difference.

126
00:10:24,649 --> 00:10:27,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your craving is just like trying to interfere, but again,

127
00:10:28,427 --> 00:10:31,408
[SPEAKER_00]: All your craving can ever accomplish is, again, arising and ceasing.

128
00:10:31,428 --> 00:10:32,869
[SPEAKER_00]: There is indifference to your craving.

129
00:10:34,470 --> 00:10:36,551
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why our craving can never be satisfied.

130
00:10:36,571 --> 00:10:36,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

131
00:10:37,991 --> 00:10:41,233
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can never even be...

132
00:10:41,293 --> 00:10:50,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the idea, the conception of the idea of satisfaction is completely ignorant, because it just doesn't even pertain to the same domain, craving and feeling.

133
00:10:51,237 --> 00:10:51,557
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

134
00:10:51,697 --> 00:10:54,318
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were generally mixed, it means craving could affect the feeling.

135
00:10:54,718 --> 00:10:55,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can't.

136
00:10:56,739 --> 00:10:58,180
[SPEAKER_00]: There's really nothing to do with craving.

137
00:11:00,285 --> 00:11:26,279
[SPEAKER_01]: To say, like, that feeling is indifferent, is... might even be to say too much, to make feelings... it sounds like feeling does care in a little way, it's indifferent, but it's... it's so... you know, like, to say something is ind... to... that is indifferent, is... is almost saying too much, giving it too much of concern, even.

138
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:29,641
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so absolutely indifferent.

139
00:11:31,105 --> 00:11:33,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I guess, I guess, depending on how you start designating terms.

140
00:11:34,006 --> 00:11:37,366
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when I say different, I mean, I mean, like, equanimous.

141
00:11:37,386 --> 00:11:40,227
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, and that's what equanimity means.

142
00:11:40,247 --> 00:11:43,568
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean I acknowledge you, but I don't care about it.

143
00:11:43,588 --> 00:11:48,029
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, like, you just... Not even... No, not... Yeah, exactly.

144
00:11:48,269 --> 00:11:53,270
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible to even acknowledge you, and that means, like, unshakable equanimity is.

145
00:11:54,971 --> 00:12:00,792
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize that aggregates cannot be acknowledged, nor can they acknowledge, because they're actively

146
00:12:01,465 --> 00:12:03,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-enclosed, indifferent to each other.

147
00:12:05,268 --> 00:12:14,334
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just the idea of them relating in any form can only arise from not knowing that they cannot relate in the first place.

148
00:12:14,354 --> 00:12:16,076
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you assume the relation.

149
00:12:16,236 --> 00:12:19,738
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why upādāna takes out the aggregates through its own assumption.

150
00:12:19,978 --> 00:12:25,442
[SPEAKER_00]: But they remain indifferent to it, through and through, for the whole duration.

151
00:12:26,803 --> 00:12:28,945
[SPEAKER_00]: They can never actually be accessed or affected.

152
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:35,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So craving is like the unnecessary pressure of jumping around?

153
00:12:35,682 --> 00:12:36,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

154
00:12:38,743 --> 00:12:40,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Unnecessary pressure, yeah.

155
00:12:40,484 --> 00:12:42,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Unnecessary lean.

156
00:12:42,644 --> 00:12:49,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Unnecessary kind of inclination.

157
00:12:54,228 --> 00:12:57,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, in the same sense, you can measure your craving because you're feeling.

158
00:12:57,890 --> 00:12:58,670
[SPEAKER_00]: You can measure your

159
00:12:59,955 --> 00:13:01,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering because of craving.

160
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:07,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneously present, inseparable.

161
00:13:09,343 --> 00:13:24,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is pain present on account of something, it's not actually on account of that thing, but it's an account of the craving that's present, as it happens to be in regard to that thing.

162
00:13:25,536 --> 00:13:27,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, somebody says something that upsets you.

163
00:13:28,279 --> 00:13:32,341
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not upset because what they said, what they've done, whatever, what they implied.

164
00:13:32,882 --> 00:13:39,165
[SPEAKER_00]: You're upset because, in regard to that experience as a whole, you are craving.

165
00:13:42,888 --> 00:13:45,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Since it's unpleasant, you're craving it to not be.

166
00:13:45,689 --> 00:13:50,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop craving it to not be, the pain will disappear.

167
00:13:51,373 --> 00:13:53,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Right there and then, that very moment.

168
00:13:53,674 --> 00:14:06,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why often people get some relief through saying, oh, just be in the present moment, or just accept things, or just open up and don't resist, and so on and so forth.

169
00:14:08,163 --> 00:14:09,924
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not enough.

170
00:14:09,945 --> 00:14:12,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can do that once, twice, a hundred times.

171
00:14:13,367 --> 00:14:15,828
[SPEAKER_00]: You can do the whole life and you might have a more peaceful life.

172
00:14:16,429 --> 00:14:18,790
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not enough to bring understanding.

173
00:14:20,909 --> 00:14:41,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, every religion teaches that, or very often, not every, maybe, but quite often, you hear that, through various gurujis or whatever else, so just be present, just don't resist, just accept, acceptance, and love, just love, like Christian love is a form of that kind of acceptance, you just love whatever comes your way and stuff.

174
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:46,062
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, that is a rather, like, overriding, that's overshooting view.

175
00:14:47,243 --> 00:14:48,724
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you should not accept everything.

176
00:14:49,024 --> 00:14:50,625
[SPEAKER_00]: You should accept only what you resist.

177
00:14:52,755 --> 00:14:55,076
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you don't resist, you shouldn't be accepting further.

178
00:14:55,716 --> 00:15:02,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't apply the blanket acceptance, because there are different types of craving.

179
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:05,181
[SPEAKER_00]: So, craving is subtler than that.

180
00:15:07,522 --> 00:15:15,845
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you were to understand fully, correctly, with the right view, what craving is, then that would be the end of it.

181
00:15:16,786 --> 00:15:19,447
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't have to do it, because now you understood it, and now it's gone.

182
00:15:21,004 --> 00:15:29,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason you have to each time accept things, once they have arisen, is because there is no understanding in relation to their arising.

183
00:15:30,627 --> 00:15:38,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you still assume that they can be craved, therefore you crave, therefore you override it through acceptance, and you let it diminish, and then you feel better.

184
00:15:39,450 --> 00:15:41,651
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like a psychological cycle.

185
00:15:43,743 --> 00:15:51,129
[SPEAKER_00]: The dhamma should take you to the point, and should be developed to such an extent, whereby you don't have to do anything in order to be free, or in order to not suffer.

186
00:15:51,689 --> 00:15:56,392
[SPEAKER_00]: That was always... that was always something that guided me, like, just... it always made sense.

187
00:15:56,472 --> 00:15:58,454
[SPEAKER_00]: I had that, even as a layperson, when I started.

188
00:15:58,514 --> 00:16:02,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, if it's a true peace, it's something that you don't have to maintain.

189
00:16:04,737 --> 00:16:09,264
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's a true freedom from suffering, it's something that doesn't require me to keep doing it.

190
00:16:09,605 --> 00:16:14,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, I have to keep doing it until you reach it, but once it's fully developed, you don't have to...

191
00:16:14,632 --> 00:16:18,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anything that was falling short of that cannot be the ultimate truth.

192
00:16:18,819 --> 00:16:19,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if...

193
00:16:19,980 --> 00:16:28,388
[SPEAKER_00]: If they say in, like, psychology or whatever, well, when the pain arises or painful memory or something happens, well, you should do this and then you should do that and you should do this.

194
00:16:28,408 --> 00:16:31,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, it helps in that linear psychological way.

195
00:16:32,531 --> 00:16:37,456
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you have to do something in the first place, that's already a problem.

196
00:16:39,658 --> 00:16:41,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, people don't see that, usually.

197
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:44,222
[SPEAKER_00]: They just take that for granted and then deal with the problem.

198
00:16:44,997 --> 00:16:47,838
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is that you have to deal with the problem in the first place.

199
00:16:47,919 --> 00:16:49,099
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already a big problem.

200
00:16:49,119 --> 00:16:50,280
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the biggest problem.

201
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:52,881
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what dukkha, this is, is.

202
00:16:55,863 --> 00:17:02,426
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not at ease because you know that there might be something you will need to do, you will have to do, in order to be at ease.

203
00:17:02,846 --> 00:17:04,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's making you uneasy.

204
00:17:05,107 --> 00:17:05,708
[SPEAKER_00]: In maintenance.

205
00:17:06,108 --> 00:17:06,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

206
00:17:07,969 --> 00:17:10,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's exactly the point when it comes to craving.

207
00:17:12,227 --> 00:17:15,950
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to do this and then you gently soften or release.

208
00:17:16,030 --> 00:17:25,738
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just psychological babble that is revolving around the same view that it's failing to see that the fact that you have to do anything in the first place is already a big issue.

209
00:17:25,938 --> 00:17:26,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's THE issue.

210
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:30,002
[SPEAKER_00]: All of this is circumstantial afterwards.

211
00:17:31,523 --> 00:17:37,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, there is a degree of truth in like, oh, when there is a resistance, anger, whatever.

212
00:17:38,028 --> 00:17:38,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Accepting it,

213
00:17:41,171 --> 00:17:44,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Not resisting it will cancel out the craving.

214
00:17:46,796 --> 00:17:51,601
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you can do that for the right reasons and you can do that for the wrong reasons.

215
00:17:52,476 --> 00:18:08,699
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know what the deal with the craving and the feeling in there, if you have no concept of simultaneous dependence, that teaches samupādā, you'll never see... you'll never do it for the right reasons, which is why somebody can be quite evolved in that sense, but still be a putrījana.

216
00:18:09,099 --> 00:18:11,300
[SPEAKER_00]: He's still doing the whole thing, rightly.

217
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:14,020
[SPEAKER_00]: He's doing nibbāna for the wrong reasons.

218
00:18:14,300 --> 00:18:16,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So his sense of I am is still there, unaffected.

219
00:18:16,701 --> 00:18:17,521
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't recognize it.

220
00:18:17,761 --> 00:18:18,641
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't recognize it.

221
00:18:25,756 --> 00:18:30,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, painful feeling is painful, because you're resisting.

222
00:18:30,887 --> 00:18:33,773
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, okay, the first thing, I have to stop resisting it.

223
00:18:34,815 --> 00:18:36,539
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you stop resisting it,

224
00:18:38,667 --> 00:18:39,707
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain might disappear.

225
00:18:40,467 --> 00:18:44,869
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't get fooled by that and then, okay, see, I fixed it, that's the fix.

226
00:18:44,889 --> 00:18:47,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, each time the pain arises, I'll stop resisting it.

227
00:18:47,969 --> 00:18:50,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you develop this general view that overrides everything.

228
00:18:51,010 --> 00:18:53,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes corresponds to the nature of things, sometimes doesn't.

229
00:18:54,031 --> 00:18:57,372
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, if it doesn't even once, means it's not the right view.

230
00:18:57,932 --> 00:18:59,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Right view is always the right view.

231
00:19:01,093 --> 00:19:04,954
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should not be fooled by when you... Yeah, you just stop resisting it.

232
00:19:04,974 --> 00:19:06,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You just resign to it.

233
00:19:07,881 --> 00:19:08,682
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain is gone.

234
00:19:08,962 --> 00:19:09,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

235
00:19:10,063 --> 00:19:11,464
[SPEAKER_00]: This particular pain is gone.

236
00:19:11,864 --> 00:19:15,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, how will I reach a point where it won't arise in the first place?

237
00:19:17,789 --> 00:19:25,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And accepting... See, all of those psychological methods, techniques, where you just accept it, or you do this, or you do that.

238
00:19:26,816 --> 00:19:28,958
[SPEAKER_00]: All that requires things to arise in the first place.

239
00:19:29,718 --> 00:19:35,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Between all that requires ignorance and craving in order to be able to deal with ignorance and craving, temporarily.

240
00:19:38,655 --> 00:19:43,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's not satisfactory for somebody who wants to be free.

241
00:19:43,556 --> 00:19:46,757
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it doesn't pertain to the nature of the Four Noble Truths.

242
00:19:47,977 --> 00:19:54,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Because Four Noble Truths are more than just, oh, you just accept or you just don't indulge.

243
00:19:54,319 --> 00:19:57,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Anybody can do that and see a degree of worldly benefit in it.

244
00:19:58,834 --> 00:19:58,994
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

245
00:19:59,454 --> 00:20:09,181
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to have a direct, simultaneous insight into presence of craving, to the extent the craving is present, to that extent suffering is present.

246
00:20:14,144 --> 00:20:23,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only reason accepting, in regard to pain, works, is because it cancels out the craving.

247
00:20:25,351 --> 00:20:26,552
[SPEAKER_00]: But accepting it,

248
00:20:27,813 --> 00:20:29,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, I'm welcoming it.

249
00:20:29,775 --> 00:20:31,756
[SPEAKER_00]: That's actually already overdoing it.

250
00:20:31,977 --> 00:20:33,258
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to go that far.

251
00:20:34,119 --> 00:20:36,601
[SPEAKER_00]: But because you don't know, you go that far.

252
00:20:36,641 --> 00:20:41,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And as it happens, going that far also passes the craving and cancels it out.

253
00:20:41,605 --> 00:20:44,047
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you keep keep walking much further than you have to.

254
00:20:45,770 --> 00:20:53,076
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, it happens that accepting also cancels out the craving in regard to the unpleasant feeling.

255
00:20:53,537 --> 00:21:03,085
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to understand that the presence of craving means presence of suffering, and you just abandon craving, then you don't have to accept anything, nor refuse anything.

256
00:21:04,687 --> 00:21:08,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the attitude of accepting and refusing is aimed at the thing out there.

257
00:21:11,392 --> 00:21:12,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You see what I mean?

258
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:31,582
[SPEAKER_00]: The path that you cross, the distance that you cross in an act of accepting something that bothers you, happens to also walk past the degree of craving that is somewhere there.

259
00:21:31,722 --> 00:21:33,463
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have no idea where that craving is.

260
00:21:33,843 --> 00:21:36,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just go the whole distance and then the craving disappears.

261
00:21:37,164 --> 00:21:37,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes.

262
00:21:38,544 --> 00:21:39,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Not always.

263
00:21:39,124 --> 00:21:41,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously.

264
00:21:41,685 --> 00:21:42,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the feeling might have changed.

265
00:21:43,513 --> 00:21:44,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

266
00:21:44,814 --> 00:21:46,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you might have been walking in a different direction.

267
00:21:46,915 --> 00:21:51,218
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have been thinking you're accepting the wrong thing and claiming you're applying to something different.

268
00:21:51,659 --> 00:21:52,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes...

269
00:21:52,339 --> 00:21:54,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you get it right, you get it for the wrong reasons.

270
00:21:56,042 --> 00:21:57,603
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you don't know why you got it right.

271
00:22:02,126 --> 00:22:10,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you recognize it... And then again, again, because of that, people develop this general blanket view, so then they just accept everything.

272
00:22:10,111 --> 00:22:10,852
[SPEAKER_00]: I love everyone.

273
00:22:11,795 --> 00:22:13,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I pretend that people are not bad.

274
00:22:15,718 --> 00:22:18,841
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to see that actually, it's irrelevant.

275
00:22:19,982 --> 00:22:21,363
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens to you?

276
00:22:21,403 --> 00:22:23,304
[SPEAKER_00]: It's irrelevant whether you accept it or not.

277
00:22:23,444 --> 00:22:24,245
[SPEAKER_00]: What is relevant?

278
00:22:24,385 --> 00:22:27,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you crave or not.

279
00:22:27,167 --> 00:22:39,097
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, what I was saying before is, there can be craving... sorry... accepting and craving... accepting or refusing implies craving.

280
00:22:39,963 --> 00:22:42,665
[SPEAKER_00]: But there can be craving without accepting or refusing.

281
00:22:47,329 --> 00:22:51,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't accept or refuse, as a patujana, without craving.

282
00:22:52,913 --> 00:22:55,435
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can still crave without accepting or refusing.

283
00:22:57,817 --> 00:23:02,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Because craving is more primordial than act of accepting in regard to a particular thing or refusing the particular thing.

284
00:23:06,764 --> 00:23:07,364
[SPEAKER_01]: You just know.

285
00:23:09,407 --> 00:23:12,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might do that initially, because your mind is not subtle enough.

286
00:23:12,869 --> 00:23:18,553
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is to take you to the point whereby you recognize that the craving is a problem, and that's where the solution is.

287
00:23:18,914 --> 00:23:22,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, abandoning craving, then, will prevent the problem from arising in the first place.

288
00:23:23,777 --> 00:23:27,980
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the act of accepting or refusing will not be the act of accepting or refusing through craving.

289
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:31,262
[SPEAKER_00]: It will just be agreeable, disagreeable.

290
00:23:32,904 --> 00:23:39,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, by knowing where the real problem of craving is, you won't have to pretend

291
00:23:39,552 --> 00:23:42,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And try to cover everything up and paint in your blanket view.

292
00:23:43,673 --> 00:23:44,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, no, no.

293
00:23:44,293 --> 00:23:45,174
[SPEAKER_00]: He's a good person.

294
00:23:45,214 --> 00:23:45,534
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

295
00:23:45,574 --> 00:23:46,154
[SPEAKER_00]: He means well.

296
00:23:46,234 --> 00:23:46,754
[SPEAKER_00]: He means well.

297
00:23:46,794 --> 00:23:46,954
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

298
00:23:47,594 --> 00:23:50,095
[SPEAKER_00]: He acts like a foolish idiot and it's a fact.

299
00:23:51,335 --> 00:23:55,396
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not upset on account of it, even if he's insulting you, because you don't crave.

300
00:23:58,397 --> 00:24:08,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, a recognition of someone's idiocy and stupidity aimed at you, that is offending you, that offense won't cause you suffering.

301
00:24:09,795 --> 00:24:22,522
[SPEAKER_00]: You can recognize this is disagreeable, but you won't suffer on that account because you won't crave in regard to what is disagreeable.

302
00:24:22,542 --> 00:24:26,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But... and you still don't have to accept what he's saying.

303
00:24:27,264 --> 00:24:29,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you accept it when you know it's lots of nonsense?

304
00:24:30,826 --> 00:24:32,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would you have to pretend that he's good?

305
00:24:33,768 --> 00:24:36,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So people do that because they don't know what to do.

306
00:24:39,306 --> 00:24:40,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Same way we spoke about the subha.

307
00:24:41,588 --> 00:24:52,797
[SPEAKER_00]: By not recognizing the subha-nimitta and asubha-nimitta, and dealing it at the level of significance, people need to... they overshoot the mark, and they look at the decomposed bodies and stuff.

308
00:24:53,157 --> 00:24:56,660
[SPEAKER_00]: That helps initially, in the same sense, accepting could help initially.

309
00:24:58,701 --> 00:25:04,686
[SPEAKER_00]: But only so that you can be able to recognize that, actually, the problem is way beyond this.

310
00:25:04,926 --> 00:25:07,188
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that I'm moved in the first place.

311
00:25:08,274 --> 00:25:13,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't want to have to go around and look at the pictures of decomposed bodies whenever I have lust arising.

312
00:25:14,397 --> 00:25:18,199
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's way too much lust, when I got careless, then it can help snap you back.

313
00:25:19,419 --> 00:25:20,280
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, no.

314
00:25:20,620 --> 00:25:26,883
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind needs to be established in regard to beautiful or ugly as something that is ugly, as something that is unwanted.

315
00:25:28,683 --> 00:25:30,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where dispassion is reached.

316
00:25:30,844 --> 00:25:32,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can have all the beauty in front of you.

317
00:25:34,026 --> 00:25:34,906
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is beyond it.

318
00:25:34,926 --> 00:25:36,267
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind sees it as it is.

319
00:25:37,279 --> 00:25:43,023
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you each time keep running back to the pictures in the super, you have a nice little collection, and it will work less and less.

320
00:25:44,385 --> 00:25:49,549
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's exactly this accepting attitude, like, oh, just be present and accept whatever comes your way.

321
00:25:49,949 --> 00:25:54,492
[SPEAKER_00]: It can come as a revelation to somebody who's never present and never accepted anything that comes his way.

322
00:25:55,413 --> 00:26:00,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, through accepting it, he does actually, accidentally as well, doesn't crave.

323
00:26:01,117 --> 00:26:03,940
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's only initially, because he just took the whole bulk.

324
00:26:04,240 --> 00:26:06,442
[SPEAKER_00]: But sooner or later, that will wear out.

325
00:26:07,539 --> 00:26:09,080
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will stop having that effect.

326
00:26:09,541 --> 00:26:11,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, you start craving in regard to that.

327
00:26:11,583 --> 00:26:14,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving doesn't stay always in front of you.

328
00:26:21,051 --> 00:26:31,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the same sense, as a subha always shoots the mark, if done improperly, accepting or refusing, or I refuse pleasure, would mean you deny all the pleasure.

329
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:34,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant feeling.

330
00:26:34,400 --> 00:26:36,321
[SPEAKER_00]: You think the pleasant feeling is the problem?

331
00:26:36,742 --> 00:26:37,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You deny it.

332
00:26:38,403 --> 00:26:39,943
[SPEAKER_00]: You think the painful feeling is the problem?

333
00:26:40,043 --> 00:26:40,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You accept it.

334
00:26:42,525 --> 00:26:46,747
[SPEAKER_00]: But neither painful nor pleasant feeling, nor neutral, are actually the problem.

335
00:27:07,358 --> 00:27:09,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have no craving in regard to what you feel.

336
00:27:11,101 --> 00:27:16,025
[SPEAKER_00]: If the bad feeling arises on account of what somebody said, you can see it clearly.

337
00:27:19,407 --> 00:27:20,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, what he said is right.

338
00:27:21,209 --> 00:27:22,149
[SPEAKER_00]: I was actually wrong.

339
00:27:24,491 --> 00:27:27,794
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this disagreeable feeling is the result of something wrong I've done.

340
00:27:29,435 --> 00:27:32,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Like sometimes when arahants are reprimanded by the Buddha.

341
00:27:34,783 --> 00:27:36,424
[SPEAKER_00]: But, because there won't be any craving.

342
00:27:36,444 --> 00:27:38,085
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't take it personally.

343
00:27:38,305 --> 00:27:38,726
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

344
00:27:38,846 --> 00:27:39,746
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

345
00:27:41,267 --> 00:27:43,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your behavior will just be modified, according to it.

346
00:27:43,709 --> 00:27:44,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, this is right.

347
00:27:46,350 --> 00:27:54,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if they just criticize you, and there is a disagreeable feeling on account of the words you're hearing, and you realize they have no idea what they talk about.

348
00:27:54,355 --> 00:27:56,397
[SPEAKER_00]: They're deluded, ignorant, angry person.

349
00:27:57,337 --> 00:27:57,778
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not.

350
00:27:57,898 --> 00:27:58,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, thank you, brother.

351
00:27:59,579 --> 00:27:59,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

352
00:28:00,239 --> 00:28:00,739
[SPEAKER_00]: You're weird.

353
00:28:00,860 --> 00:28:01,460
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're not.

354
00:28:02,113 --> 00:28:02,753
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a fact.

355
00:28:02,993 --> 00:28:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't want to be there.

356
00:28:03,753 --> 00:28:06,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you either dismiss them or leave yourself.

357
00:28:06,154 --> 00:28:07,434
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to deal with an idiot.

358
00:28:10,635 --> 00:28:11,395
[SPEAKER_01]: It's being praised.

359
00:28:11,536 --> 00:28:13,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you won't crave being praised.

360
00:28:13,416 --> 00:28:13,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

361
00:28:14,016 --> 00:28:14,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure.

362
00:28:14,436 --> 00:28:15,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be taken by.

363
00:28:16,817 --> 00:28:18,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes praise is a fact.

364
00:28:18,357 --> 00:28:18,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

365
00:28:18,817 --> 00:28:20,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can confirm it, as the Buddha did.

366
00:28:20,618 --> 00:28:21,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I am the greatest.

367
00:28:21,978 --> 00:28:22,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

368
00:28:23,479 --> 00:28:25,499
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes they praise you for completely wrong reasons.

369
00:28:26,539 --> 00:28:28,480
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't be intoxicated by either of it.

370
00:28:41,088 --> 00:28:48,830
[SPEAKER_01]: If I look back now, there's no intense, like, intense feelings or...

371
00:28:49,210 --> 00:28:52,611
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't notice any intense... No, no, it's a neutral feeling.

372
00:28:53,071 --> 00:28:53,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

373
00:28:54,652 --> 00:28:56,332
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the hardest one to recognize.

374
00:28:58,833 --> 00:28:59,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Greatly in regards to that.

375
00:28:59,993 --> 00:29:00,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

376
00:29:01,414 --> 00:29:08,636
[SPEAKER_00]: And as we spoke before, clearly there are indolence, like resting, feeling secure, feeling safe.

377
00:29:09,904 --> 00:29:11,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling calm, feeling peaceful.

378
00:29:13,966 --> 00:29:16,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's the peace of an arahant, then it's not the peace.

379
00:29:27,133 --> 00:29:28,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should not relax.

380
00:29:29,335 --> 00:29:32,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You can lay down and sleep, but infernally, your mind should never relax.

381
00:29:33,377 --> 00:29:34,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't need to relax.

382
00:29:35,058 --> 00:29:35,858
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

383
00:29:36,198 --> 00:29:37,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Body needs to relax.

384
00:29:37,199 --> 00:29:38,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Body needs to sleep.

385
00:29:38,120 --> 00:29:38,760
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't.

386
00:29:40,566 --> 00:29:41,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't at all.

387
00:29:47,330 --> 00:29:48,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm really tired.

388
00:29:48,591 --> 00:29:49,392
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to sit down.

389
00:29:49,932 --> 00:29:54,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You sit down, but with your body sitting down, your mind sits down.

390
00:29:55,276 --> 00:29:56,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is an excuse for that.

391
00:29:58,598 --> 00:30:04,642
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha meant when he said the consciousness should not rest and get established upon anything.

392
00:30:06,357 --> 00:30:14,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Like a cow, if it leans onto the wall, it's eaten by the insects, if it lays on the ground, if it leans on the tree, if it... it's eaten by whatever it leans on.

393
00:30:18,900 --> 00:30:20,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And that doesn't require effort, either.

394
00:30:20,401 --> 00:30:26,662
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require you like... you know, clenching your teeth, you know, like enduring.

395
00:30:26,742 --> 00:30:27,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at all.

396
00:30:27,523 --> 00:30:32,984
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just an attitude of mind in regard to laying in your bed all day.

397
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:37,181
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just having that mindfulness still.

398
00:30:37,561 --> 00:30:38,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

399
00:30:38,422 --> 00:30:42,344
[SPEAKER_00]: That attitude requires mindfulness, which is why mindfulness is always needed.

400
00:30:43,185 --> 00:30:44,285
[SPEAKER_00]: You can have too much of it.

401
00:30:45,306 --> 00:30:47,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And mindfulness, to the extent necessary... And persistence, you know.

402
00:30:47,347 --> 00:30:49,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Persistence is the biggest... Energy.

403
00:30:49,768 --> 00:30:50,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, persistence.

404
00:30:50,909 --> 00:30:51,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

405
00:30:51,930 --> 00:30:52,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Mental drilling.

406
00:30:53,371 --> 00:30:54,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Making that mental effort.

407
00:30:55,112 --> 00:30:56,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't tire you bodily.

408
00:30:56,933 --> 00:30:57,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, yeah, if...

409
00:30:58,494 --> 00:31:00,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like, you know, get up, keep walking.

410
00:31:00,555 --> 00:31:01,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just go to sleep again.

411
00:31:02,848 --> 00:31:07,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people give in to sleep as a form of... mind goes to sleep.

412
00:31:08,631 --> 00:31:12,553
[SPEAKER_00]: If... if you go to sleep only when your body really needs to sleep, that's fine.

413
00:31:13,754 --> 00:31:19,297
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you go to sleep as a psychological result of, you know, you don't want to deal with stuff or... the mind is resting.

414
00:31:19,897 --> 00:31:26,301
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I like to come back from work, open a can of beer and, you know, watch my favorite TV program.

415
00:31:27,380 --> 00:31:28,080
[SPEAKER_00]: I deserve it.

416
00:31:28,201 --> 00:31:29,781
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been working eight hours a day.

417
00:31:30,102 --> 00:31:30,842
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to rest.

418
00:31:31,402 --> 00:31:32,943
[SPEAKER_00]: That's obviously the crudest example.

419
00:31:33,764 --> 00:31:45,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can, you know, live in a kutti without a TV, without anything, and, yeah, I've been meditating for 10 hours, and now I'm gonna go and sit down and have a cup of tea and rest.

420
00:31:46,371 --> 00:31:51,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, do that, but that's still not an excuse for your mind to sit down and rest.

421
00:31:54,785 --> 00:31:58,687
[SPEAKER_00]: No is an excuse that when you are busy, for your mind to go and get busy.

422
00:32:01,688 --> 00:32:06,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I think to see the distinction, this is my body and it will do what it has to do, what it needs to do.

423
00:32:07,250 --> 00:32:10,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I walk, I think... And keep your mind alert in regard to it.

424
00:32:12,032 --> 00:32:13,773
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what mindfulness of the body means.

425
00:32:13,913 --> 00:32:16,874
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean you're like a robot touching.

426
00:32:16,894 --> 00:32:21,096
[SPEAKER_00]: It means basically you're mindful and aware of whatever your body is doing throughout the day and night.

427
00:32:22,528 --> 00:32:25,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Actions, intentions and stuff, you're mindful in regard to it.

428
00:32:26,269 --> 00:32:27,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't lay in it.

429
00:32:27,770 --> 00:32:28,750
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't come into it.

430
00:32:29,550 --> 00:32:30,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't become it.

431
00:32:35,673 --> 00:32:40,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I would say, like, a good... a good night or a good day, so... you... you contemplate and contemplate.

432
00:32:41,675 --> 00:32:47,597
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you're laying down... Yeah, you... you can give up.

433
00:32:47,717 --> 00:32:48,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop contemplating now.

434
00:32:48,917 --> 00:32:50,037
[SPEAKER_01]: You've been working all day.

435
00:32:50,177 --> 00:32:51,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it will continue, kind of...

436
00:32:52,174 --> 00:32:55,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether if it continues or not, that's fine.

437
00:32:55,115 --> 00:32:55,955
[SPEAKER_00]: That's on its own.

438
00:32:56,956 --> 00:33:02,418
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the only thing you should be concerned when you're, okay, I've been making effort all day, I can't do it anymore.

439
00:33:02,738 --> 00:33:03,278
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

440
00:33:04,299 --> 00:33:05,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and have a break.

441
00:33:06,299 --> 00:33:08,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically and mentally, so to speak.

442
00:33:08,660 --> 00:33:10,841
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't allow your mind to rest.

443
00:33:11,881 --> 00:33:16,483
[SPEAKER_00]: In that rest.

444
00:33:16,503 --> 00:33:19,584
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, always keep an eye on it.

445
00:33:19,844 --> 00:33:21,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that doesn't require... that requires zero effort.

446
00:33:22,101 --> 00:33:25,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the I, the awareness, the degree of awareness, is always there.

447
00:33:26,224 --> 00:33:27,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you'd be dead.

448
00:33:28,046 --> 00:33:31,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So you... that's why mindfulness comes with understanding of what mindfulness is.

449
00:33:34,951 --> 00:33:35,952
[SPEAKER_00]: The awareness is always there.

450
00:33:37,053 --> 00:33:38,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean, oh, the awareness is always there.

451
00:33:39,014 --> 00:33:39,174
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

452
00:33:39,454 --> 00:33:43,197
[SPEAKER_00]: The degree of awareness is always there for as long as you're alive.

453
00:33:43,377 --> 00:33:45,219
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how aggregates work.

454
00:33:48,966 --> 00:33:53,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just means that whatever is arising, there is a degree of awareness in regard to it.

455
00:33:53,589 --> 00:33:55,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, that thing will not be manifested.

456
00:33:56,211 --> 00:33:57,912
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you stop doing the awareness.

457
00:33:58,012 --> 00:33:58,452
[SPEAKER_00]: I am aware.

458
00:33:58,472 --> 00:33:59,072
[SPEAKER_00]: I am mindful.

459
00:33:59,413 --> 00:34:02,475
[SPEAKER_00]: You just recognize it as coming, being implied in the things.

460
00:34:03,875 --> 00:34:05,496
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind never forgets that.

461
00:34:05,737 --> 00:34:09,239
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that way, you never lose mindfulness, even in the act of forgetting.

462
00:34:10,460 --> 00:34:13,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You forget something particularly, but you don't forget the nature of forgetting.

463
00:34:13,902 --> 00:34:14,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

464
00:34:25,439 --> 00:34:28,300
[SPEAKER_00]: And that resting, that welcoming, is delighting.

465
00:34:29,481 --> 00:34:30,481
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what delighting is.

466
00:34:31,181 --> 00:34:31,701
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

467
00:34:31,761 --> 00:34:33,442
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't delight, it doesn't welcome.

468
00:34:34,823 --> 00:34:35,543
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you have to do.

469
00:34:38,064 --> 00:34:42,286
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been sleeping all day, and wake up, and you still feel like crap, and you can't get up.

470
00:34:43,346 --> 00:34:44,706
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just physically wiped.

471
00:34:46,687 --> 00:34:48,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you go back to sleep.

472
00:34:49,428 --> 00:34:53,550
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine, as long as you don't welcome it, internally.

473
00:34:54,475 --> 00:34:56,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome anything internally.

474
00:34:56,677 --> 00:34:58,539
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean you have to stop doing it externally.

475
00:35:00,821 --> 00:35:01,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a distinction.

476
00:35:04,104 --> 00:35:05,045
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't welcome it.

477
00:35:07,928 --> 00:35:13,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And through that non-welcoming, if it was genuinely bodily tiredness, yeah, you'll sleep.

478
00:35:13,933 --> 00:35:15,775
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it ain't, it will become apparent.

479
00:35:16,728 --> 00:35:28,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm not actually sleeping anymore, which means it wasn't bothering, it was something my mind wanted to do, because there was no other distractions to keep me up, engaged, so I just couldn't deal with boredom.

480
00:35:30,170 --> 00:35:33,031
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when you're bored, fine, we're bored, don't do anything.

481
00:35:33,891 --> 00:35:34,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome that.

482
00:35:36,471 --> 00:35:40,132
[SPEAKER_00]: You can keep not doing anything, but never welcome it for a second.

483
00:35:40,252 --> 00:35:45,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can keep doing, but, oh, I want to lay down, I want to go for a nice pleasant walk, climb the waterfalls.

484
00:35:46,801 --> 00:35:47,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fine.

485
00:35:47,261 --> 00:35:47,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it.

486
00:35:47,921 --> 00:35:49,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it in the beginning.

487
00:35:49,062 --> 00:35:50,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it at the end.

488
00:35:50,322 --> 00:35:53,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it for any time in between.

489
00:35:54,424 --> 00:35:56,585
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind always remains alert in regard to it.

490
00:35:57,345 --> 00:35:58,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's effortless.

491
00:35:59,105 --> 00:36:00,006
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require any effort.

492
00:36:00,706 --> 00:36:04,267
[SPEAKER_00]: In the beginning, obviously, you feel like, you know, spoiling the party.

493
00:36:04,807 --> 00:36:08,009
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just because you're overshooting the mark, initially.

494
00:36:08,029 --> 00:36:08,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're all uptight.

495
00:36:10,369 --> 00:36:10,970
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not want this.

496
00:36:11,010 --> 00:36:11,690
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not want this.

497
00:36:11,770 --> 00:36:12,530
[SPEAKER_00]: I must not want this.

498
00:36:13,431 --> 00:36:14,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, don't welcome that.

499
00:36:33,239 --> 00:36:37,161
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the craving for being and non-being is just that.

500
00:36:38,602 --> 00:36:39,563
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the feeling, eh?

501
00:36:42,305 --> 00:36:42,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

502
00:36:44,186 --> 00:36:47,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for being, craving for non-being and for sensuality.

503
00:36:48,049 --> 00:36:52,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, craving for sensuality is craving for being or non-being through the senses.

504
00:36:53,413 --> 00:36:54,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's even more proliferate.

505
00:36:55,914 --> 00:36:58,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for a thing, for something to be... For a particular thing.

506
00:36:59,417 --> 00:37:03,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for a particular this or a particular that, means central craving.

507
00:37:03,679 --> 00:37:15,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you can stop craving in regard to a particular object, particular objects of your senses, and then you abandon the central craving, but then you still have the more subtle craving for being and the absence of being, non-being.

508
00:37:16,903 --> 00:37:21,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for a phenomenon to be or a phenomenon to disappear.

509
00:37:21,726 --> 00:37:26,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still a subtle kind of affinity and resistance.

510
00:37:28,690 --> 00:37:36,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, much subtler than actually fully developed craving for sensual objects or absence of them, if they are unpleasant.

511
00:37:37,636 --> 00:37:38,856
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still the same nature.

512
00:37:39,056 --> 00:37:41,518
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why anāgāmī is not free from those two types of craving.

513
00:37:47,950 --> 00:38:06,243
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't love and hate, which pertains to the infatuation or revulsion in regard to central objects, but you can still experience craving in regard to phenomena and absence.

514
00:38:06,303 --> 00:38:09,065
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean craving for the old hidden lights in the dhamma.

515
00:38:11,126 --> 00:38:11,727
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

516
00:38:12,808 --> 00:38:16,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhādeva was affected by that woman laughing at him.

517
00:38:17,326 --> 00:38:20,347
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't hate her, because he was affected by it.

518
00:38:20,647 --> 00:38:26,709
[SPEAKER_00]: He craved the non-being of that experience, which is what snapped him out and made him realize.

519
00:38:35,891 --> 00:38:44,854
[SPEAKER_00]: And say, like when anāgāmīs don't, lay anāgāmīs, when they don't become bhikkhus or bhikkhunis and go forth, is when they still

520
00:38:47,046 --> 00:38:50,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they crave for the notion of the sense of duty in regard to their family.

521
00:38:52,672 --> 00:38:55,275
[SPEAKER_00]: They... No, that's the right thing.

522
00:38:55,315 --> 00:38:56,056
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I want to do.

523
00:38:56,276 --> 00:38:58,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So they stay to look after their parents.

524
00:38:58,899 --> 00:39:00,240
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why they don't become Arhats.

525
00:39:01,181 --> 00:39:02,583
[SPEAKER_00]: But they abandon sensuality, you know.

526
00:39:28,253 --> 00:39:32,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't see your craving directly.

527
00:39:33,615 --> 00:39:35,816
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, there, so now I'll stop doing it.

528
00:39:38,577 --> 00:39:47,621
[SPEAKER_00]: No, what you have to watch in the back of your mind is whether you delight in delight in an acceptance or delight in aversion.

529
00:39:49,362 --> 00:39:49,742
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all.

530
00:39:50,673 --> 00:39:52,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Anger offers a form of pleasure.

531
00:39:55,134 --> 00:40:10,937
[SPEAKER_00]: He says something, and you feel angry, and then you delight in telling them off, or revenging, payback, or ranting about them to somebody else, which gives you full relief, like, yeah, yeah, it's like this, it's like that, it's like that, it's like that.

532
00:40:11,937 --> 00:40:13,097
[SPEAKER_00]: You're delighting in that.

533
00:40:17,678 --> 00:40:18,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to not crave,

534
00:40:19,477 --> 00:40:22,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can know for sure, if there is a suffering present, craving is there.

535
00:40:24,140 --> 00:40:26,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you indirectly, directly do it?

536
00:40:27,922 --> 00:40:28,602
[SPEAKER_00]: If you know what I mean.

537
00:40:28,622 --> 00:40:35,065
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an indirect action, but if it's fully known how it can be done, it can be done directly, in its indirect nature.

538
00:40:36,386 --> 00:40:41,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, well, watching the light.

539
00:40:41,349 --> 00:40:42,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you watch the light.

540
00:40:42,970 --> 00:40:43,910
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something you do.

541
00:40:44,410 --> 00:40:47,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm welcoming it, therefore I will not welcome it.

542
00:40:48,347 --> 00:40:50,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Through not welcoming, you cannot crave it.

543
00:40:50,909 --> 00:40:53,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Through not craving, the suffering won't be there.

544
00:40:54,512 --> 00:41:04,181
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you also need to recognize that, because the degree of displeasure will still be there, but that's not what suffering is.

545
00:41:05,562 --> 00:41:11,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the understanding is the, like, the core of the whole thing, and the reason for liberation.

546
00:41:12,488 --> 00:41:16,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody can do this, but without understanding,

547
00:41:17,051 --> 00:41:25,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Without being able to discern what he's told to discern, I discern the pain, arisen on its own, from the suffering.

548
00:41:27,539 --> 00:41:30,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you discern that, it is the same thing.

549
00:41:30,321 --> 00:41:32,483
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically like two sheets of glass, identical.

550
00:41:32,723 --> 00:41:35,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you know that there are two, you won't know that there are two.

551
00:41:35,565 --> 00:41:37,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's just one thing.

552
00:41:39,328 --> 00:41:42,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no difference between the two.

553
00:41:42,911 --> 00:41:43,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Except that there are two.

554
00:41:45,032 --> 00:41:45,893
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference.

555
00:41:50,092 --> 00:41:51,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Too much suffering and pain.

556
00:41:51,473 --> 00:41:52,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

557
00:41:53,134 --> 00:41:54,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Physical pain, it means.

558
00:41:54,575 --> 00:42:00,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, see, that when you say physical pain, people usually associate bodily sensation.

559
00:42:00,579 --> 00:42:01,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's unpleasant.

560
00:42:01,519 --> 00:42:02,960
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's not physical pain.

561
00:42:03,020 --> 00:42:03,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Annoyance.

562
00:42:04,781 --> 00:42:10,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Hearing somebody shouting abuse at you is a physical pain, because it came through your physical ears.

563
00:42:12,526 --> 00:42:15,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing a disgusting image is a physical pain.

564
00:42:17,329 --> 00:42:18,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's pain that is in regard to what

565
00:42:20,147 --> 00:42:23,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, having a lynch chopped off is also physical pain.

566
00:42:26,949 --> 00:42:30,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, mental pain is in regard to any of that type of pain.

567
00:42:33,911 --> 00:42:35,632
[SPEAKER_00]: And your mind is affected by it as well.

568
00:42:40,774 --> 00:42:45,075
[SPEAKER_01]: This is in general pain, if you're unpleasant.

569
00:42:46,151 --> 00:42:50,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there is the feeling of, am I bothered on account of feeling unpleasant?

570
00:42:52,513 --> 00:42:54,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I feel an urge to deal with it?

571
00:42:55,674 --> 00:42:56,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, oh, I'm craving.

572
00:42:57,935 --> 00:43:00,135
[SPEAKER_00]: There is craving present, and that's the reason why I suffer.

573
00:43:00,435 --> 00:43:03,156
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem, not the unpleasant feeling that has arisen.

574
00:43:04,337 --> 00:43:08,838
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it says, like, he develops three, four knowledge, and

575
00:43:10,093 --> 00:43:17,735
[SPEAKER_00]: becomes an arahant, and he recognizes, any disturbance that is left for me now, will be disturbance on account of this five senses, on account of this body.

576
00:43:18,755 --> 00:43:24,576
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's often said, ah, just endure unpleasant, unpleasant words and abuse, or whatever, done.

577
00:43:24,976 --> 00:43:26,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Just endure it, Brahman, you're, you're done.

578
00:43:27,417 --> 00:43:28,237
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that to Angulimala.

579
00:43:28,597 --> 00:43:31,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would Buddha need to say that to Angulimala, when he was already an arahant?

580
00:43:32,218 --> 00:43:33,238
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you would think, wouldn't you?

581
00:43:34,923 --> 00:43:35,924
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you're just reminding him.

582
00:43:36,244 --> 00:43:38,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is... there will be still this pleasure.

583
00:43:38,846 --> 00:43:48,732
[SPEAKER_00]: But none of it can ever, even remotely, conceive the pain, like suffering, which is what pain is.

584
00:43:48,812 --> 00:43:50,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Disagreeable feeling is not pain.

585
00:43:52,294 --> 00:43:57,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wanting disagreeable feeling, that's what pain is.

586
00:43:57,357 --> 00:44:04,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you could overshoot, as you say, by feeling a cramp in your leg or knee, starting to twist or

587
00:44:07,049 --> 00:44:07,710
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll endure it.

588
00:44:07,850 --> 00:44:08,490
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll endure it.

589
00:44:08,791 --> 00:44:10,232
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already misplacing.

590
00:44:10,332 --> 00:44:11,653
[SPEAKER_00]: You're thinking it's out there.

591
00:44:12,313 --> 00:44:12,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to endure?

592
00:44:12,934 --> 00:44:13,134
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

593
00:44:14,615 --> 00:44:15,596
[SPEAKER_00]: What you should do then?

594
00:44:16,317 --> 00:44:25,144
[SPEAKER_00]: You should move and then recognize the pain or the guilt that arises on account that you couldn't hold up your determination to not move.

595
00:44:25,844 --> 00:44:34,651
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where suffering is more likely to be found than enduring in your twisted joint and then turning yourself into an invalid because you're such a keen meditator.

596
00:44:39,565 --> 00:44:48,307
[SPEAKER_00]: The anxiety, that hollow feeling that arises on the fact that you could not hold up a determination that has a reason, I will not move.

597
00:44:48,667 --> 00:44:49,287
[SPEAKER_00]: I moved.

598
00:44:50,387 --> 00:44:51,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, what does that show you?

599
00:44:51,807 --> 00:44:52,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You were not in control.

600
00:44:53,667 --> 00:44:54,848
[SPEAKER_00]: That's very unpleasant.

601
00:44:55,028 --> 00:44:55,908
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what suffering is.

602
00:44:59,869 --> 00:45:01,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let that arise.

603
00:45:01,129 --> 00:45:08,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't bother torturing your body and twisting your knees and, you know, pushing the nails through your skin or whatever else they do.

604
00:45:09,943 --> 00:45:18,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, let the guilt, let the anxiety, let those painful mental feelings arise, because they all have in common suffering.

605
00:45:18,348 --> 00:45:19,329
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no doubt about that.

606
00:45:19,949 --> 00:45:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You can suffer in regard to the bodily, what you heard, what you did.

607
00:45:23,931 --> 00:45:28,814
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it comes to this type of suffering, that is always suffering, which is why those things cannot arise in another hand.

608
00:45:29,515 --> 00:45:33,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Disagreeable feeling can arise on account of somebody shouting abuse at him.

609
00:45:35,418 --> 00:45:38,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Agreeable feeling might arise on account of somebody praising him.

610
00:45:39,798 --> 00:45:44,060
[SPEAKER_00]: None of those things will induce lust or aversion anymore, ever again.

611
00:45:44,460 --> 00:45:52,363
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, you'll never ever experience anxiety, guilt, doubt, and all those mental painful feelings.

612
00:45:53,743 --> 00:45:55,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is that difference between mental and bodily.

613
00:45:56,384 --> 00:45:59,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, it just means, on what account has it arisen?

614
00:46:00,066 --> 00:46:01,807
[SPEAKER_00]: But every feeling is a mental feeling.

615
00:46:03,227 --> 00:46:04,948
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't feel feelings in your body.

616
00:46:06,146 --> 00:46:07,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I feel something in my back.

617
00:46:07,546 --> 00:46:07,766
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

618
00:46:08,086 --> 00:46:09,407
[SPEAKER_00]: You perceive something in your back.

619
00:46:09,547 --> 00:46:11,827
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel it in regard to your whole body.

620
00:46:11,867 --> 00:46:14,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a particular aspect of your body.

621
00:46:14,448 --> 00:46:16,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm about to lose my hand.

622
00:46:16,608 --> 00:46:17,789
[SPEAKER_00]: I feel very scared now.

623
00:46:18,189 --> 00:46:19,909
[SPEAKER_00]: And very, very afraid.

624
00:46:24,110 --> 00:46:24,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what hurts.

625
00:46:26,591 --> 00:46:31,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the perception of whatever's happening in your hand.

626
00:46:31,752 --> 00:46:34,793
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't feel any physical pain.

627
00:46:35,193 --> 00:46:35,793
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was painful.

628
00:46:44,423 --> 00:46:53,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the body is, you know, rational.

629
00:46:53,669 --> 00:46:57,111
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to keep moving here and there, you know.

630
00:46:57,331 --> 00:47:01,174
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's annoying.

631
00:47:02,034 --> 00:47:02,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the problem.

632
00:47:04,376 --> 00:47:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: I try not to scratch, I just try to

633
00:47:12,807 --> 00:47:21,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might initially not want to scratch, just because, oh, you're not sure where to look for the craving, or where the problem is.

634
00:47:22,450 --> 00:47:24,530
[SPEAKER_00]: But the problem is not the need.

635
00:47:24,770 --> 00:47:29,612
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not the arising of the itch and the scratch.

636
00:47:31,072 --> 00:47:33,453
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is whether you imply craving through doing it.

637
00:47:35,453 --> 00:47:40,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether action constantly implies craving, in which case it's an action, it's a karma.

638
00:47:41,643 --> 00:47:44,484
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove craving formation, then karma ceases to be karma.

639
00:47:44,804 --> 00:47:48,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's just a response to things.

640
00:47:49,465 --> 00:47:51,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Significance, intentions.

641
00:47:52,906 --> 00:47:58,347
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

642
00:48:02,889 --> 00:48:04,409
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be careful in doing things.

643
00:48:04,529 --> 00:48:06,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you should put up with things.

644
00:48:07,130 --> 00:48:08,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything you become stronger,

645
00:48:11,076 --> 00:48:13,117
[SPEAKER_01]: But you haven't... that doesn't mean that you've gone beyond.

646
00:48:13,937 --> 00:48:14,737
[SPEAKER_00]: On the survey, no.

647
00:48:14,937 --> 00:48:15,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Something else comes along.

648
00:48:16,238 --> 00:48:16,378
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

649
00:48:17,138 --> 00:48:27,901
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's why, like in the sutta, some monks, you know, needed ascetic practices, because that would be making them genuinely look at the right direction, in the right way.

650
00:48:28,641 --> 00:48:29,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Some didn't.

651
00:48:29,722 --> 00:48:31,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Some actually was completely counterproductive.

652
00:48:33,243 --> 00:48:37,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Through ascetic practices, for them, they were overshooting the mark, and they could not see it.

653
00:48:48,592 --> 00:48:52,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why applying the blanket of, we should all be doing this, we should all be doing that, is ridiculous.

654
00:48:55,497 --> 00:49:01,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, to have to wait for something big to happen, or do something really dramatic to see how it happens.

655
00:49:01,961 --> 00:49:15,830
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, naturally, things will happen that are quite dramatic, so to speak, or quite intense, or you lose a limb, or whatever.

656
00:49:16,530 --> 00:49:23,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Or even if that doesn't happen, the fact that it can happen, if you dwell on it, that will become dramatic in itself.

657
00:49:23,311 --> 00:49:24,572
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how people lose their minds.

658
00:49:25,852 --> 00:49:31,513
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't lose their minds when painful things happen to them directly.

659
00:49:31,733 --> 00:49:32,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

660
00:49:32,393 --> 00:49:45,376
[SPEAKER_00]: They lose their minds when they start, gradually, like they get eroded by the recognition of the possibility that they're completely exposed, at any given time, at any given second, to absolutely anything.

661
00:49:46,113 --> 00:49:46,733
[SPEAKER_00]: It freaks you out.

662
00:49:47,494 --> 00:49:49,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when something bad happens, it's right in front of you.

663
00:49:49,735 --> 00:49:51,556
[SPEAKER_00]: It's painful, whatever, but it happened.

664
00:49:52,937 --> 00:49:53,818
[SPEAKER_01]: But when there's something...

665
00:49:53,838 --> 00:49:54,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Anticipation.

666
00:49:54,678 --> 00:49:55,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

667
00:49:55,739 --> 00:49:56,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Anticipation.

668
00:49:57,300 --> 00:50:01,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is what it's meant in the sutta as being praying to suffering.

669
00:50:02,243 --> 00:50:03,564
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't actually suffer.

670
00:50:04,965 --> 00:50:06,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you don't have to suffer.

671
00:50:06,546 --> 00:50:09,888
[SPEAKER_00]: But you recognize that you are praying

672
00:50:10,990 --> 00:50:14,692
[SPEAKER_00]: To suffering, like, descending upon you, so to speak, for whatever reasons.

673
00:50:15,092 --> 00:50:15,272
[SPEAKER_00]: You...

674
00:50:15,733 --> 00:50:17,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine, how would you feel if you were a prey?

675
00:50:18,634 --> 00:50:28,700
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't a problem in front of you, but you just feel like, at any time, something can pounce on you and hunt you down and get you like that.

676
00:50:29,661 --> 00:50:34,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if nothing does, the fact that this... that's what brings you... makes you mad.

677
00:50:35,604 --> 00:50:38,406
[SPEAKER_00]: The anticipation in the war, like, when people are in war,

678
00:50:39,794 --> 00:50:52,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Anticipate... that's when, you know, they go gray and lose their, lose their, you know, age 50 years overnight, because of their weight of, oh, I might die.

679
00:50:55,177 --> 00:51:04,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's much easier when the fight breaks, start, oh, it's right there in front, so you just fight or not, but it's the anticipation of the fight.

680
00:51:04,899 --> 00:51:05,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, it's the same nature.

681
00:51:06,100 --> 00:51:07,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Anticipation of the suffering.

682
00:51:08,735 --> 00:51:10,556
[SPEAKER_00]: He feels he's prey to suffering.

683
00:51:12,297 --> 00:51:13,097
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the scary.

684
00:51:13,277 --> 00:51:14,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the fact that you'll suffer.

685
00:51:14,838 --> 00:51:15,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's also painful.

686
00:51:15,779 --> 00:51:17,500
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you can suffer.

687
00:51:17,520 --> 00:51:19,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can suffer again and again and again.

688
00:51:19,861 --> 00:51:20,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will.

689
00:51:25,044 --> 00:51:25,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is...

690
00:51:25,824 --> 00:51:28,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine if there is...

691
00:51:28,845 --> 00:51:30,146
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are a prey to something.

692
00:51:30,246 --> 00:51:33,648
[SPEAKER_00]: If something is hunting you constantly.

693
00:51:36,983 --> 00:51:43,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's that as well.

694
00:51:43,188 --> 00:51:49,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to contemplate that.

695
00:51:49,492 --> 00:51:49,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

696
00:51:49,893 --> 00:52:01,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might try to bring it up in particular things, and you might bring it up to an extent in regard to particular things, but you kind of leave yourself out of it.

697
00:52:02,044 --> 00:52:04,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that sort of incomplete contemplation.

698
00:52:05,347 --> 00:52:17,418
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start thinking about the fact that you'll die, stop breathing, drown, it's risky, because it can really freak you out.

699
00:52:18,058 --> 00:52:20,480
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, it does hammer it back on.

700
00:52:21,661 --> 00:52:22,562
[SPEAKER_01]: As you so are.

701
00:52:22,982 --> 00:52:24,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That you are liable to death.

702
00:52:25,184 --> 00:52:28,027
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't understood.

703
00:52:28,167 --> 00:52:29,048
[SPEAKER_00]: If you fear death,

704
00:52:30,498 --> 00:52:33,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, ah, there is a fear in regard to something like which means there is something you're still holding.

705
00:52:34,579 --> 00:52:35,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that degree, you're liable.

706
00:52:36,140 --> 00:52:42,022
[SPEAKER_01]: But it gives you the... Well, it kind of... ...the motivation.

707
00:52:42,202 --> 00:52:47,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Motivation, it sort of, like, delineates, delineates, that's the word, isn't it?

708
00:52:47,644 --> 00:52:49,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Delineates, outlines the problem.

709
00:52:50,085 --> 00:52:52,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Reminds you of where the problem is.

710
00:52:57,288 --> 00:52:58,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you shouldn't cut lines.

711
00:52:59,729 --> 00:53:02,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, you know, that's what dhamma is, that's what dhamma is.

712
00:53:02,470 --> 00:53:08,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And you deal with that problem, you overcome death, fear of death, of anxiety.

713
00:53:10,353 --> 00:53:14,795
[SPEAKER_00]: No particular problem arising on account of a particular instance, you know, can ever bother you.

714
00:53:22,598 --> 00:53:24,219
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just gonna be disagreeable, agreeable.

715
00:53:24,459 --> 00:53:27,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Ultimately, you left it all.

716
00:53:34,257 --> 00:53:49,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, if you're imagining linearly, in a process, then it's not really akālika.

717
00:53:51,742 --> 00:53:58,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it can always be akālika, in the sense that any linear process is present as such, as an adjacent thing.

718
00:53:59,325 --> 00:54:03,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And mindful of that means, ah, right there, right now, so that is akālika.

719
00:54:04,557 --> 00:54:10,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no matter what stage of your linear contemplation of death, you recognize, I will die.

720
00:54:12,083 --> 00:54:12,964
[SPEAKER_00]: That's simultaneous.

721
00:54:25,895 --> 00:54:33,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not doing the contemplation so to actually die, but doing contemplation to allow that fear of death to arise.

722
00:54:34,617 --> 00:54:38,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And see on what level it arises and then realize this is what needs to be dealt with.

723
00:54:39,718 --> 00:54:48,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even that fear, if it's unpleasant, it's not unpleasant because of the dying and because of the fear, it's unpleasant because you crave and you so don't want it.

724
00:54:50,521 --> 00:54:54,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So you remove that not wanting it.

725
00:54:54,162 --> 00:54:55,963
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't welcome the unwanting.

726
00:54:58,664 --> 00:55:00,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you won't be afraid of death either.

727
00:55:04,112 --> 00:55:08,175
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing you'll be, is very mindful, if the death comes.

728
00:55:09,216 --> 00:55:10,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even just the thought of it.

729
00:55:10,617 --> 00:55:14,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Or just the thought of death will immediately bring the mindfulness, because that's where mindfulness comes from.

730
00:55:15,020 --> 00:55:21,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, like Sotāpanna has been negligent throughout life, the moment of death, he will realize the fruit.

731
00:55:21,584 --> 00:55:22,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where the fruit is.

732
00:55:30,610 --> 00:55:31,811
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm trying again to...

733
00:55:33,255 --> 00:55:36,016
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, because I know...

734
00:55:36,136 --> 00:55:39,118
[SPEAKER_01]: I do know that there's a possibility.

735
00:55:39,158 --> 00:55:41,819
[SPEAKER_01]: I leave the situation, I get caught up in another situation.

736
00:55:41,879 --> 00:55:44,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I go out of the city or whatever, then I get caught up in a situation.

737
00:55:44,780 --> 00:55:45,861
[SPEAKER_01]: I can still get caught up.

738
00:55:47,221 --> 00:55:50,443
[SPEAKER_01]: I pick up a phone, I can fill a pool or whatever.

739
00:55:51,723 --> 00:55:55,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes I don't see the craving

740
00:55:59,408 --> 00:56:05,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why you have to have that wisdom to recognize, oh, the problem is still there.

741
00:56:05,849 --> 00:56:15,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether I'm now using this environment to avoid the recognition and reminders that the problem is still there, or whether I'm using it to really practice.

742
00:56:16,951 --> 00:56:20,632
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, it's like how you eat your food and why you eat your food.

743
00:56:21,252 --> 00:56:26,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you allow it to become because it's essential, or whether you keep in mind the real reasons.

